# Everything, on one page

::::box{class=lead}
HyMarkX is small enough that its documentation fits in a single document. Every example below
is compiled by this page — the rendered half is the real component, not a screenshot.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #quick-start}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
From nothing to a page
::::

## Quick start

::::box{class=lead}
Six steps, each one a command you can paste. By the end you will have a page you can put on any
static host, and you will have watched the JavaScript appear only when you asked for it.
::::

::::box{class=step-block}

### 1 · Install it

```sh
npm install hymarkx
```

Installs the `hmx` command into the current project. `npm install -g hymarkx` if you would
rather have it on your PATH everywhere.

::::

::::box{class=step-block}

### 2 · Write a file

Nothing here is HyMarkX yet — it is a Markdown file, and that is the point.

```md
# Release notes

Shipping on **Friday**.
```

Save it as `index.md`. A file you already have works just as well.

::::

::::box{class=step-block}

### 3 · Build it

```sh
npx hmx build index.md --out dist
```

You get `dist/index.html`: a complete document with `<!doctype>`, a `<title>` taken from
frontmatter if you wrote any, and nothing else. No CSS file, because nothing used any. No
JavaScript file, because nothing needed any.

::::

::::box{class=step-block}

### 4 · Add something Markdown cannot say

```md
# Release notes

:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::

Shipping on **Friday**.
```

Build again. Now there is a `dist/index.css` beside the HTML, holding the styles for `note` and
for nothing else. Delete the note, build again, and the file goes away.

::::

::::box{class=step-block}

### 5 · See it while you write

```sh
npx hmx dev .
```

Serves the directory with live reload, so the page updates as you save. `npx hmx check .`
reports diagnostics without writing anything, which is the form to put in CI, and
`npx hmx fmt .` formats HyMarkX constructs while leaving your prose alone.

::::

::::box{class=step-block}

### 6 · Put it somewhere

`dist/` is plain files. Any static host will serve it — Vercel, Netlify, GitHub Pages, S3, a
directory behind nginx. There is no server half and no runtime to install: this site is built
by the same command and deployed exactly this way.

::::

::::box{class=lead}
Interactivity is the one thing that needs a decision from you rather than from the document.
`::state` and event handlers only compile under `--trust app`, the mode a host picks for content
it wrote itself — see [trust modes](#stability) below and the
[interactivity guide](https://github.com/NAME0x0/HyMarkX/blob/main/docs/guides/interactivity.md).
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #components}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
The built-in vocabulary
::::

## Components

::::box{class=lead}
Nine components ship with the language. Each one is a container directive: three colons, a
name, optional attributes in braces, and a matching closing fence.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::card[Quarterly report]
Revenue rose in every region except EMEA.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::card[Quarterly report]
Revenue rose in every region except EMEA.
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::metric[requests per second]
18,400
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::metric[requests per second]
18,400
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
A release is :badge[stable]{kind=success} once green.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

A release is :badge[stable]{kind=success} once green.
:::

::::

::::box{class=lead}
The rest are `grid` for columns, `box` for a plain wrapper — give it `as=section` or `as=nav`
when the wrapper means something — and `button`, `input` and `form`, which exist so that
interactivity has native elements to attach to.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #values}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
Frontmatter and expressions
::::

## Values

::::box{class=lead}
Frontmatter is YAML, parsed with aliases bounded and custom tags disabled. Read it back with
double braces. Everything resolves at compile time, so a document that only interpolates ships
no JavaScript.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
---
region: EMEA
target: 12000
---

Sales in {{ region }} against a target of {{ target }}.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

Sales in EMEA against a target of 12000.
:::

::::

::::box{class=lead}
Expressions are deliberately not JavaScript. They can compare, do arithmetic, and choose
between values — and they cannot reach the network, storage, timers, or any host object. That
restriction is what makes it safe to compile a document you did not write.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #interactivity}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
State and events
::::

## Interactivity

::::box{class=lead}
Declare state in the document root, assign it from an allowed event, and read it anywhere. The
initial value is already in the HTML, so the page is correct before the runtime loads — and the
runtime is 591 bytes, emitted only when something uses it.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
::state{count=0}

:::button{on-click="count = count + 1"}
Increment
:::

Clicked {{ count }} times.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

A live example needs `--trust app`; this page is built in `document` mode, so the source is
shown without running it. The [gallery](/gallery#controls) and the [home page](/#how) have it
running.
:::

::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #styling}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
Scoped CSS
::::

## Styling

::::box{class=lead}
A component may carry a `<style scoped>` block. Its selectors are rewritten to match only the
elements that component emitted, and `:global(...)` opts a rule back out. Rules are emitted
once per component, however many times it is used.
::::

```md
<style scoped>
.callout { border-left: 3px solid currentColor; }
:global(body) { font-family: system-ui; }
</style>
```

::::box{class=lead}
Built-in components bring a restrained default stylesheet, and only for the components a
document actually uses. Override it with design tokens — `--hmx-color-info`, `--hmx-radius`
and the rest — rather than by fighting selectors.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #diagnostics}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
When something is wrong
::::

## Diagnostics

::::box{class=lead}
Nothing recoverable throws. Every problem is a diagnostic carrying a stable code, a severity and
a real span, and one renderer draws them all — so the compiler, the CLI, the language server and
the playground say the same words about the same characters.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::note[Heads up]{typ=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=pane}
### What you are told

```text
warning[HMX2001]: Unknown attribute "typ" on component "note".
  ┌─ notes.hmx:1:19
  │
1 │ :::note[Heads up]{typ=warning}
  │                   ^^^
  │
help: type
```
:::

::::

::::box{class=lead}
The code is the part that lasts. `HMX2001` means the same thing in every version, so it is worth
suppressing, searching for, or asserting on in a test — while the wording is free to improve.
Severity decides what happens next: `hmx check` fails the build on an error and reports the rest.
::::

::::box{class=lead}
The [playground](/play) renders diagnostics with this same function, on whatever you type. It is
the fastest way to find out what a code means.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #stability}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
What alpha means here
::::

## Stability

::::box{class=lead}
The version is 0.0.9. Pre-1.0, syntax may change, and the specification says so rather than
implying otherwise: every change is recorded in an architecture decision record and the
changelog, and there are no migration paths yet. What follows is which parts have actually
moved and which are held down by something.
::::

| Area | Status | What holds it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Plain Markdown and GFM | **Stable** | 652 CommonMark and 40 GFM examples run on every commit. A document with no HyMarkX construct compiling differently is a failed build, not a footnote. |
| Trust modes | **Stable** | Normative in the specification: the host selects the mode, and no construct in a document can raise it. |
| Diagnostic codes | **Stable** | A code is stable once published and never reused. A test fails if a code is emitted without being documented, or documented without being emitted. |
| Directive syntax | **Settled, moved once** | `:::name[label]{attrs}` has not changed shape, but 0.0.9 changed how containers nest and made code fences opaque (ADR-0021). Additive, and still a syntax change inside one alpha. |
| Built-in components | **Will grow** | Nine ship today. Adding one cannot break a document; defaults can be tuned — 0.0.5 changed which attributes a declared prop consumes (ADR-0019). |
| Frontmatter | **Reserved keys will grow** | `title`, `description`, `layout`, `lang`, `draft`, `canonical`, `icon`, `image`, `siteName` and `author` are reserved at this version. A key added later could collide with one of yours. |
| Expressions | **Bounded by design** | They can compare, do arithmetic and choose between values. They cannot reach the network, storage, timers or any host object, and that is a security boundary rather than a roadmap item. |
| State and events | **Newest, most likely to move** | Named derived state is not specified at this version. |
| Authored components | **Incomplete** | Named slots are not specified at this version. |
| Styling by untrusted authors | **Not available** | A document compiled in `document` trust cannot bring its own styles. |

::::box{class=lead}
Practical version of all that: pin an exact version rather than a range, read the changelog
before upgrading, and keep your documents in source control — which, since they are text files
that render as Markdown anywhere, they already are.
::::

:::note[Not for untrusted content in production]{type=warning}
Compiling a document from a stranger is safe by design — expressions cannot reach a host object,
and `document` trust is the default. It is not yet *proven* safe: this is alpha software whose
security work is written down, not finished. The
[security notes](https://github.com/NAME0x0/HyMarkX/blob/main/SECURITY.md) and the
[audit](https://github.com/NAME0x0/HyMarkX/blob/main/docs/security-audit.md) name every control
and the ones with no test behind them.
:::

::::

